1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
Our prototype takes a short text prompt and a random seed.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:11,700
It turns them into a small plan, expands that plan into seventy-two thousand colored shapes called Jewels, and renders forty-nine frames.

3
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:14,640
No finished video is supplied at generation time.

4
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:19,440
The path really runs from words to a plan to a field of Jewels to moving pixels.

5
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:24,223
A system can appear creative while quietly copying most of its answer.

6
00:00:24,223 --> 00:00:25,564
We block that shortcut.

7
00:00:25,564 --> 00:00:29,355
Generation receives only the prompt and the declared random seed.

8
00:00:29,355 --> 00:00:35,946
It does not receive an input video, a fitted version of the target, a hidden target plan, or a saved target code.

9
00:00:35,946 --> 00:00:43,820
Some building blocks still come from training examples; that is an important limit, but the test video itself never enters the process.

10
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:47,242
Think of the first stage as a tiny recipe writer.

11
00:00:47,242 --> 00:00:55,019
It recognizes one of the three prompts the prototype currently knows, then uses the seed to choose two different training-owned ingredients.

12
00:00:55,019 --> 00:00:59,963
One ingredient will supply the moving subject and the other will supply the surroundings.

13
00:00:59,963 --> 00:01:05,240
The same prompt and seed always produce the same recipe, which makes every result reproducible.

14
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:09,737
The recipe does not list seventy-two thousand Jewels one by one.

15
00:01:09,737 --> 00:01:14,068
It names a scene, a subject ingredient, a setting ingredient, and a seed.

16
00:01:14,068 --> 00:01:19,288
Those names act like instructions for whole regions of the video, not individual pixels.

17
00:01:19,288 --> 00:01:27,060
This hierarchy matters: the short recipe keeps the subject consistent across time, while the many Jewels provide the visual detail.

18
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:31,502
Imagine drawing the subject's path through a stack of video frames.

19
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:35,746
The path forms a tube: a connected region that moves through space and time.

20
00:01:35,746 --> 00:01:40,661
We take the thirty-six thousand Jewels closest to that tube from the subject ingredient.

21
00:01:40,661 --> 00:01:44,682
We take thirty-six thousand far-away Jewels from the setting ingredient.

22
00:01:44,682 --> 00:01:48,480
The result has an exact size and a clear division of responsibility.

23
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:55,004
Each Jewel has a continuous position, a shape choice, a base-color choice, and a color-change choice.

24
00:01:55,004 --> 00:02:00,481
Continuous means its center can land anywhere inside the volume, not only at fixed grid points.

25
00:02:00,481 --> 00:02:04,171
A small random nudge prevents repeated positions from lining up.

26
00:02:04,171 --> 00:02:10,860
The output is therefore an irregular cloud of soft colored shapes rather than a blocky three-dimensional pixel grid.

27
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,380
The renderer turns the field into a normal video.

28
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:22,380
In nine matched tests, the intended prompt was identified eight times, and correct prompts beat wrong or empty prompts in every comparison.

29
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:25,891
That is real evidence that text controls the generated field.

30
00:02:25,891 --> 00:02:33,258
It is not yet a general text-to-video model: the vocabulary contains only three prompts and eighteen training-owned ingredients.

31
00:02:33,258 --> 00:02:35,560
We have proved the route, not the scale.
